## What happened

In **.NET 11**, Apple removed DSA support from macOS's native cryptography libraries, and .NET followed suit. `DSA.Create()` and all DSA operations now **throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS**. Windows and Linux are unaffected.

## How to fix

Since you only *verify* legacy DSA signatures, you have two options:

### Option 1 — Platform guard (keep verifying on Windows/Linux only)

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    dsa.ImportParameters(legacyPublicKey);
    bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);
}
else
{
    // Use a cross-platform crypto library (e.g., BouncyCastle)
    // or skip verification on macOS if acceptable.
}
```

### Option 2 — Replace with a managed implementation (recommended)

Use **BouncyCastle** (`Portable.BouncyCastle` or `BouncyCastle.Cryptography` NuGet package) for DSA verification on all platforms:

```csharp
// Works everywhere, no platform dependency
var pubKey = new DsaPublicKeyParameters(y, new DsaParameters(p, q, g));
var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
signer.Init(false, pubKey);
signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = signer.VerifySignature(signature);
```

### Summary

| Platform | .NET 10 | .NET 11 |
|----------|---------|---------|
| Windows  | ✅ Works | ✅ Works |
| Linux    | ✅ Works | ✅ Works |
| macOS    | ✅ Works | ❌ Throws |

If you need cross-platform DSA verification, switch to a managed crypto library like BouncyCastle. If you're generating new signatures, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** instead.
